1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,367
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. Every AI system that produces an answer

2
00:00:03,367 --> 00:00:06,700
eventually needs a score attached to it — a confidence percentage, a

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:07,767
pass or fail, a grade.

4
00:00:08,533 --> 00:00:11,933
Here's the question almost nobody asks before trusting that number: who, or

5
00:00:11,933 --> 00:00:14,333
what, actually produced it — and can that thing be trusted more

6
00:00:14,333 --> 00:00:17,333
than the system it's grading? Today: a teardown of grading the machine

7
00:00:17,333 --> 00:00:18,133
itself.

8
00:00:18,167 --> 00:00:21,167
The obvious answer — ask the AI how confident it is,

9
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:24,100
or ask a second AI to check the first one's work —

10
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:26,000
turns out to be broken in a specific,

11
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:30,400
well-documented way. And the fix is less glamorous than it sounds: mostly

12
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,467
boring, structural, computed arithmetic.

13
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:34,967
Which turns out to be exactly why it works.

14
00:00:35,730 --> 00:00:37,697
The simplest approach is also the most tempting:

15
00:00:38,063 --> 00:00:40,530
just have the model tell you how sure it is. Append a

16
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:42,463
confidence score to the output — 'I'm 92%

17
00:00:42,863 --> 00:00:45,597
confident in this conclusion' — and use that number downstream.

18
00:00:46,163 --> 00:00:49,297
Here's the problem, and it's not minor: the same mechanism that produced

19
00:00:49,297 --> 00:00:52,997
a wrong answer is the mechanism producing the confidence score attached to

20
00:00:52,997 --> 00:00:53,797
it.

21
00:00:53,497 --> 00:00:56,597
A model that confidently hallucinated a number has no separate,

22
00:00:57,097 --> 00:01:00,997
more-honest module standing off to the side, watching itself, immune to the

23
00:01:00,997 --> 00:01:02,097
exact same failure.

24
00:01:02,463 --> 00:01:03,363
It's the same fluent,

25
00:01:03,763 --> 00:01:05,297
pattern-completing process either way.

26
00:01:05,797 --> 00:01:09,663
Self-reported confidence measures how confident the text sounds. It does not measure

27
00:01:09,663 --> 00:01:13,697
how well-founded the underlying claim actually is. Those two things correlate

28
00:01:13,697 --> 00:01:14,730
loosely at best.

29
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:18,450
So the next instinct is: fine, don't let it grade itself —

30
00:01:18,483 --> 00:01:21,817
have a second, independent AI model review the first one's reasoning and

31
00:01:21,817 --> 00:01:22,917
judge whether it holds up.

32
00:01:23,417 --> 00:01:25,083
This sounds like it should fix the problem.

33
00:01:25,550 --> 00:01:28,083
It doesn't — because a highly capable judge model is just as

34
00:01:28,083 --> 00:01:29,117
good at recognizing a

35
00:01:29,583 --> 00:01:32,883
well-written, coherent-sounding piece of reasoning as it is at recognizing a

36
00:01:33,117 --> 00:01:33,917
poorly-written one.

37
00:01:33,983 --> 00:01:36,683
But 'well-written and coherent' was never the actual question.

38
00:01:37,283 --> 00:01:40,850
The question was: is this reasoning genuine, or a fluent, after-the-fact

39
00:01:40,883 --> 00:01:43,917
rationalization for a conclusion the model already committed to?

40
00:01:44,283 --> 00:01:48,583
A judge model evaluating narrative plausibility can't tell those two apart, because

41
00:01:48,583 --> 00:01:51,983
both look identical from the outside — both are fluent text that

42
00:01:51,983 --> 00:01:53,217
reads like sound reasoning.

43
00:01:53,750 --> 00:01:56,783
Handing the grading job to a second language model doesn't add a

44
00:01:56,783 --> 00:01:57,583
layer of

45
00:01:57,750 --> 00:02:01,283
truth-checking. It adds a second opinion from something with the exact same

46
00:02:01,283 --> 00:02:03,417
category of blind spot as the first.

47
00:02:04,083 --> 00:02:08,083
This isn't hypothetical — it's the reason serious systems that considered this

48
00:02:08,083 --> 00:02:09,550
approach walked away from it.

49
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,460
So if you can't trust the model to grade itself, and you

50
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:15,193
can't fully trust a second model to grade it either, what's left?

51
00:02:15,727 --> 00:02:16,527
Something much less

52
00:02:16,827 --> 00:02:18,293
impressive-sounding, and much harder to fool:

53
00:02:18,693 --> 00:02:22,227
a confidence score computed from structural facts about the run, not generated

54
00:02:22,227 --> 00:02:25,160
as another piece of text by an AI at all. Start every

55
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:26,393
run at a baseline score.

56
00:02:26,827 --> 00:02:31,360
Then apply fixed, programmatic penalties for specific, observable conditions — a

57
00:02:31,393 --> 00:02:34,493
data source that came back simulated instead of live, a source that

58
00:02:34,493 --> 00:02:37,627
failed to fetch at all, a structural parse failure that required a

59
00:02:37,627 --> 00:02:41,827
retry. None of these penalties are opinions. They're deductions applied by ordinary

60
00:02:41,827 --> 00:02:45,927
code, checking ordinary facts about what actually happened, with no language model

61
00:02:45,927 --> 00:02:48,993
anywhere in the scoring loop. This score is deliberately boring.

62
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:52,060
It doesn't know anything about whether the reasoning was clever or the

63
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:56,160
writing was persuasive — it only knows whether the underlying data quality

64
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:58,960
held up, and whether the process executed cleanly.

65
00:02:59,527 --> 00:03:02,793
That narrowness is the entire point: it can't be talked into a

66
00:03:02,793 --> 00:03:06,427
higher score by fluent prose, because fluent prose was never an input

67
00:03:06,427 --> 00:03:07,293
to the calculation.

68
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:10,480
Here's the part that's easy to get wrong even once you've built

69
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:11,780
a real scoring mechanism:

70
00:03:12,213 --> 00:03:14,747
what do you do when the score comes back bad? The tempting

71
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:17,847
move is to quietly suppress or soften a low-confidence result — don't

72
00:03:17,847 --> 00:03:20,580
show the user a shaky answer, or bury the caveat in fine

73
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:22,213
print. That's exactly backwards.

74
00:03:22,713 --> 00:03:23,513
A suppressed

75
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:26,913
low-confidence run protects nobody — it just trades a visible warning for

76
00:03:26,913 --> 00:03:30,147
silent incompetence, and the user gets an answer with no idea it

77
00:03:30,147 --> 00:03:30,947
was shaky.

78
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:33,047
Every run, no matter how low it scores,

79
00:03:33,447 --> 00:03:37,613
still gets delivered, with an unmissable, unambiguous header stating it's uncertain.

80
00:03:38,247 --> 00:03:41,180
The system telling you plainly that it wasn't sure is itself the

81
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:42,080
safety mechanism.

82
00:03:42,513 --> 00:03:45,480
A footnote doesn't count. It has to be impossible to miss.

83
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:48,767
Here's the part a lot of teardowns like this one skip, because

84
00:03:48,767 --> 00:03:50,867
it's less satisfying than a clean resolution:

85
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:55,067
the specific penalty values in a computed scoring system — exactly how

86
00:03:55,067 --> 00:03:58,200
much to deduct for a simulated source versus a failed one —

87
00:03:58,233 --> 00:04:02,167
are, at first, educated guesses, argued from first principles, not yet measured

88
00:04:02,167 --> 00:04:03,533
against real-world outcomes.

89
00:04:04,033 --> 00:04:05,633
That's not a footnote to bury either.

90
00:04:06,233 --> 00:04:10,400
An uncalibrated penalty model is quietly shaping every confidence score the system

91
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:11,200
produces,

92
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:14,467
for every run with a data quality issue, and nobody yet knows

93
00:04:14,467 --> 00:04:16,167
for certain if those numbers are right.

94
00:04:16,767 --> 00:04:18,667
The honest position isn't 'we solved grading.'

95
00:04:19,167 --> 00:04:21,067
It's 'we replaced an opinion with an equation,

96
00:04:21,433 --> 00:04:24,133
and now we owe ourselves the work of checking whether the equation's

97
00:04:24,133 --> 00:04:28,400
constants are actually correct' — a task that only becomes possible once

98
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:31,800
you've moved to something computed and inspectable in the first place.

99
00:04:32,433 --> 00:04:35,600
You cannot calibrate a number that was never written down as a

100
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:36,567
number to begin with.

101
00:04:37,130 --> 00:04:39,197
So here's what actually holds up, and what doesn't.

102
00:04:39,597 --> 00:04:43,197
Self-graded confidence: biased, because the same process makes the mistake and

103
00:04:43,197 --> 00:04:43,997
grades it.

104
00:04:44,363 --> 00:04:48,063
AI-judged: a category error, because a good rationalization and genuine reasoning

105
00:04:48,063 --> 00:04:49,597
read identically from the outside.

106
00:04:49,997 --> 00:04:53,163
Computed: inspectable, and still being calibrated — the only one of the

107
00:04:53,163 --> 00:04:55,497
three where a mistake in the score is something you can actually

108
00:04:55,497 --> 00:04:58,397
go find, and fix, because you can see exactly how it was

109
00:04:58,397 --> 00:04:59,197
built.

110
00:04:59,330 --> 00:05:02,297
Grading the machine was never about finding a score you can blindly

111
00:05:02,297 --> 00:05:05,897
trust. It's about finding a score whose mistakes you can find and

112
00:05:05,897 --> 00:05:06,697
fix.

113
00:05:06,797 --> 00:05:09,897
That's a lower bar than 'guaranteed correct' — and it's the only

114
00:05:09,897 --> 00:05:13,030
version of that bar that's honest about what a confidence score can

115
00:05:13,030 --> 00:05:13,830
actually promise.

116
00:05:14,590 --> 00:05:15,390
So:

117
00:05:15,123 --> 00:05:18,823
self-graded confidence is biased, because the same process makes the mistake and

118
00:05:18,823 --> 00:05:19,623
grades it.

119
00:05:19,957 --> 00:05:23,257
A second AI judge is a category error — a good rationalization

120
00:05:23,257 --> 00:05:25,623
and genuine reasoning read identically from outside.

121
00:05:26,023 --> 00:05:30,023
A computed score, built from fixed deductions on observable facts, is inspectable

122
00:05:30,123 --> 00:05:31,857
— and it's the only one of the three where you can

123
00:05:31,857 --> 00:05:35,257
actually go find a mistake and fix it. It's still being calibrated.

124
00:05:35,523 --> 00:05:36,623
That's the honest state of it.

125
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:40,347
Your turn. Find one place your own system produces a confidence score

126
00:05:40,347 --> 00:05:41,147
or a grade.

127
00:05:41,547 --> 00:05:44,613
Ask: is it self-reported by the same model that produced the answer?

128
00:05:45,013 --> 00:05:47,447
Is it graded by a second model judging plausibility?

129
00:05:47,813 --> 00:05:50,113
Or is it computed from fixed, observable deductions?

130
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:53,513
If it's either of the first two, sketch what an inspectable, computed

131
00:05:53,513 --> 00:05:56,347
version would actually look like for your case — even three or

132
00:05:56,347 --> 00:05:57,913
four penalty rules is a start.

133
00:05:58,530 --> 00:06:00,663
That question — can you trust the thing doing the checking —

134
00:06:00,663 --> 00:06:04,063
comes up again next, in a very different shape: what happens when

135
00:06:04,063 --> 00:06:07,530
the content an agent is checking is quietly giving it instructions of

136
00:06:07,530 --> 00:06:08,330
its own.

137
00:06:08,430 --> 00:06:09,230
Signing off — Divij Pawar.
